<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0008585> rdfs:label "HELLP syndrome"@en ;
    dcterms:description "A life-threatening condition that can potentially complicate pregnancy. It is named for 3 features of the condition: H emolysis, E levated L iver enzyme levels, and L ow P latelet levels. It typically occurs in the last 3 months of pregnancy (the third trimester) but can also start soon after delivery. A wide range of non-specific symptoms may be present in women with HELLP syndrome. Symptoms may include fatigue; malaise; fluid retention and excess weight gain; headache; nausea and vomiting; pain in the upper right or middle of the abdomen; blurry vision; and rarely, nosebleed or seizures. The cause of HELLP syndrome is not known, but certain risk factors have been associated with the condition. It is most common in women with preeclampsia or eclampsia. If not diagnosed and treated quickly, HELLP syndrome can lead to serious complications for the mother and baby.The main treatment is to deliver the baby as soon as possible, even if premature. Treatment may also include medications needed for the mother or baby, and blood transfusion for severe bleeding problems." ;
    ns1:category ns1:Disease ;
    ns1:exact_synonym "hemolysis-elevated liver enzymes-low platelets syndrome",
        "hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets in pregnancy"@en,
        "hemolysis-elevated liver enzymes-low platelet count syndrome"@en ;
    ns1:related_synonym "toxaemia of pregnancy",
        "PEE1"@en,
        "PREECLAMPSIA/eclampsia 1"@en,
        "hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, lowered platelets"@en ;
    ns1:synonym "PEE1",
        "PREECLAMPSIA/eclampsia 1",
        "hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets in pregnancy"@en,
        "hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, lowered platelets"@en,
        "hemolysis-elevated liver enzymes-low platelet count syndrome"@en,
        "hemolysis-elevated liver enzymes-low platelets syndrome"@en,
        "toxaemia of pregnancy"@en ;
    ns1:xref "ICD10CM:O14.2",
        "MEDGEN:58162",
        "MESH:D017359",
        "NCIT:C84750",
        "icd11.foundation:1748922908",
        "DOID:13133"@en,
        "EFO:0007297"@en,
        "GARD:0008528"@en,
        "MedDRA:10049058"@en,
        "Orphanet:244242"@en,
        "UMLS:C0162739"@en .

<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0012061> rdfs:label "familial sick sinus syndrome" ;
    dcterms:description "Sick sinus syndrome is a rare cardiac rhythm disease, usually of the elderly, characterized by electrocardiographic findings of sinus bradycardia, atrial fibrillation, atrial tachycardia sinus arrest, or sino-atrial block, and that manifest with symptoms like syncope, dizziness, palpitations, fatigue, or even heart failure. It results from malfunction of the cardiac conduction system, probably secondary to degenerative fibrosis of nodal tissue in the elderly or secondary to cardiac disorders in younger patients." ;
    ns1:category ns1:Disease ;
    ns1:exact_synonym "hereditary sick sinus syndrome",
        "familial sinus node dysfunction"@en ;
    ns1:related_synonym "SSS1",
        "sick sinus syndrome 1, autosomal recessive"@en ;
    ns1:synonym "hereditary sick sinus syndrome",
        "SSS1"@en,
        "familial sinus node dysfunction"@en,
        "sick sinus syndrome 1, autosomal recessive"@en ;
    ns1:xref "MEDGEN:573766",
        "MESH:C563907",
        "MedDRA:10040639",
        "OMIMPS:608567",
        "icd11.foundation:1495462959",
        "GARD:0013663"@en,
        "Orphanet:166282"@en,
        "SCTID:233913007"@en,
        "UMLS:C0340491"@en .

<http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0015280> rdfs:label "cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Cardiofaciocutaneous (CFC) syndrome is a RASopathy characterized by craniofacial dysmorphology, congenital heart disease, dermatological abnormalities (most commonly hyperkeratotic skin and sparse, curly hair), growth retardation and intellectual disability." ;
